WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:10.010
RL: The work done by a cell is often not visible to the human eye.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:13.930
So to make it more meaningful to us, we can consider the analogous work

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:15.920
we might do as humans.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.810
So consider a boulder lying at the bottom of a hill.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.770
We can imagine having to exert a lot of energy

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:24.370
to push that boulder to the top of the hill.

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:26.710
If we then release the boulder at any point

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:28.910
before getting it all the way to the top,

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:32.790
it will roll back downhill without needing any help from us.

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:35.360
Processes like a boulder rolling down a hill,

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:39.160
that happen without having to add any additional effort or energy,

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.280
are called spontaneous.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.960
Processes like pushing a boulder up a hill,

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.610
that requires the input of energy, are called non-spontaneous.

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:51.450
Many cellular processes are spontaneous.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.960
For example, in the last lesson, we described

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.800
how phospholipids self-assemble into bilayers

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.780
when dissolved in an aqueous solution such as water or the cytosol.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:03.610
We can now further define this self-assembly

00:01:03.610 --> 00:01:06.100
as being a spontaneous process.

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:10.410
On the other hand, when a cell has to perform a non-spontaneous process,

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:13.340
such as making a protein, it typically does so

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:17.890
by releasing the energy stored in a molecule called ATP.

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:21.530
Since ATP is used to power so many cellular functions,

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:26.750
it is vitally important for a cell to be able to produce large amounts of ATP.

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:30.450
With this in mind, let's examine one organelle in particular,

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.190
the mitochondrion, one of my favorites.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:35.470
As we mentioned briefly in the last lesson,

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:37.460
the mitochondrion is the functional module

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:40.390
that allows cells to maximize the amount of ATP that

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:45.600
is generated from the energy contained in the food we and our cells consume.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.060
Compartmentalization is crucial.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.570
Here, we're looking at a three-dimensional transmission

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:56.980
EM of a cell, contrary to the 2D representation that we usually see.

00:01:56.980 --> 00:02:00.340
The compartmentalization is clearer and more evident.

00:02:00.340 --> 00:02:05.120
The role of the mitochondrion is quite specifically to synthesize ATP.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.169
Each mitochondrion is surrounded by a double membrane structure made up

00:02:09.169 --> 00:02:12.150
of separate outer and inner membranes.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:16.070
The outer mitochondrial membrane contains porin channels.

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.550
These are channels that allow ions on most small molecules

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:21.820
to diffuse into the mitochondrion.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:25.300
Macromolecules, such as proteins and RNA molecules,

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.670
are way too large to diffuse through the outer membrane.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:32.840
The outer membrane is also extremely rich in enzymes associated

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.440
with mitochondrial lipid synthesis.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:38.620
This is critical for the division of mitochondria-- indeed,

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:41.720
their reproduction-- because to create more mitochondria,

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.510
you need more membrane.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:46.900
In contrast, the inner mitochondrial membrane

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:51.750
is highly folded into invaginations called cristae.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.050
And it surrounds the innermost compartment

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:56.750
of the mitochondrion, called the matrix.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:01.120
Cardiolipin is an abundant lipid in the mitochondrial inner membrane.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:04.470
However, it is not present in the mitochondrial outer membrane,

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:08.310
which is primarily composed of regular phospholipids.

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:12.970
This helps to underscore just how different those two membranes are.

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:15.430
Unlike the outer membrane, the inner membrane

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:19.170
is highly impermeable to most small ions.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:26.310
It is composed of three major classes of proteins that make up 76% of its mass.

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:29.400
The first class of proteins are those enzymes

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:35.230
critical for catalyzing a subset of reactions necessary for metabolism.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:38.270
The second class are transport proteins that

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:42.470
help move metabolites, like pyruvates, from the intermembrane space

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:45.010
into the matrix of the mitochondria.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:48.160
And the third class are a highly specialized set

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.860
of enzyme complexes called the F1F0ATPase that

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.550
directly synthesizes ATP.

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:58.920
Given the relative difficulty of moving molecules

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.190
across the inner mitochondrial membrane and into the matrix,

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:06.880
specific transporter proteins are required for moving small molecules

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.100
and ions across this membrane.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:12.280
The mitochondrion's double membranes establish

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:16.250
two discrete and distinctive areas within the organelle.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:20.459
We saw that the matrix is the volume enclosed by the inner membrane,

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:23.590
making it the innermost space within the mitochondrion,

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:25.940
while the volume between the two membranes

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:28.430
is called the intermembrane space.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:32.280
The matrix contains hundreds of enzymes involved

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:35.340
in what's called the citric acid cycle.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.040
And it also contains the mitochondrial DNA genome.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.580
It is a far more viscous environment compared to the cytosol,

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:47.470
because it has a higher concentration of soluble proteins.

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:50.450
It plays a critical role in ATP synthesis,

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:54.580
and in the process of so-called oxidative phosphorylation.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:58.880
The intermembrane space has a similar consistency as the cytosol.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.100
However, the protein composition is very different due to size selectivity

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:05.780
of the outer mitochondrial membrane.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:07.790
There are also proteins that are specifically

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:10.400
localized in the intermembrane space.

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:16.110
Cytochrome c is one such example of a protein concentrated in this region.

